<section class="section section--cream">
  <div class="container" style="max-width:760px">
    <div class="text-center">
      <span class="section-label">Common questions</span>
      <h2 class="section-title" style="margin-inline:auto">Questions about hourly work</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="faq-list" style="margin-top:2rem">
      <div class="faq-item">
        <button class="faq-btn" aria-expanded="false">What does $100 an hour buy? <span class="faq-icon" aria-hidden="true"></span></button>
        <div class="faq-answer"><div class="faq-answer-inner">The same standards as every site on the plan: custom code, accessibility, speed, and a written description of the finished work. The rate is flat, the same for the second hour as the 20th, and the estimate names the total before any work starts.</div></div>
      </div>
      <div class="faq-item">
        <button class="faq-btn" aria-expanded="false">Why is this hourly instead of part of the plan? <span class="faq-icon" aria-hidden="true"></span></button>
        <div class="faq-answer"><div class="faq-answer-inner">The $175-a-month plan is sized for a typical service-business website: 10 pages, a blog, hosting, and ongoing updates. Some work, such as custom calculators, outside integrations, and platform moves, falls outside that. Hourly pricing keeps small jobs small and prices big jobs by the hours they take instead of a guess, and the plan stays at $175 either way.</div></div>
      </div>
      <div class="faq-item">
        <button class="faq-btn" aria-expanded="false">Can I lock in a block of hours in advance? <span class="faq-icon" aria-hidden="true"></span></button>
        <div class="faq-answer"><div class="faq-answer-inner">Yes. Once you have a written estimate, the <a href="/subscribe/#hourly" class="inline-link">hourly checkout</a> on the sign-up page handles a one-time block of hours. The block matches the estimate exactly, and any unused balance is refundable.</div></div>
      </div>
      <div class="faq-item">
        <button class="faq-btn" aria-expanded="false">What if the work runs over the estimate? <span class="faq-icon" aria-hidden="true"></span></button>
        <div class="faq-answer"><div class="faq-answer-inner">If the work is going to exceed the estimate, I tell you before it does, with a written reason and a revised total. You can approve it, reduce the scope, or stop, so you never get a surprise invoice.</div></div>
      </div>
      <div class="faq-item">
        <button class="faq-btn" aria-expanded="false">What if I'd rather talk than fill in a form? <span class="faq-icon" aria-hidden="true"></span></button>
        <div class="faq-answer"><div class="faq-answer-inner">For a brand-new website, the <a href="/contact/" class="inline-link">free call</a> is the right place to start. For an addition to an existing site, this form is faster, and we can have a short call about scope if the work calls for one.</div></div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

